# OpenReply scheduled jobs. Install to /etc/cron.d/openreply (mode 0644, root).
#
# Replaces the three Vercel crons in vercel.json, which stop running the moment
# DNS points at this box, plus the new retention purge.
#
# Times are UTC — the host timezone is set to UTC during provisioning. They are
# spread an hour apart on purpose: on a 1 GB box with 1/8 OCPU, two of these
# running at once is a real memory and CPU spike.
#
# Output goes to root's mail / the journal. Each job exits non-zero on an HTTP
# error (curl --fail), so a silent failure is not possible.

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

# m  h  dom mon dow  user  command

# Nightly database dumps, before the purge runs so a backup always predates it.
30  3  *   *   *    root  /home/ubuntu/openreply/deploy/backup-openreply.sh

# Delete records past their retention window (lib/ops/retention.ts).
20  4  *   *   *    root  /home/ubuntu/openreply/deploy/run-cron-job.sh purge-retention

# Refresh Instagram long-lived tokens nearing expiry. This one is the lifeline:
# if it stops running, the token eventually expires and every send fails.
20  5  *   *   *    root  /home/ubuntu/openreply/deploy/run-cron-job.sh refresh-tokens

# Bind "next reel" campaigns to a newly published reel. Vercel's free plan capped
# this at once a day; nothing stops it running hourly here if you want campaigns
# to go live faster.
20  6  *   *   *    root  /home/ubuntu/openreply/deploy/run-cron-job.sh attach-next-reel

# Record one follower total per account per day. Instagram retains only ~30 days
# of insights, so a missed run loses that day permanently.
20  7  *   *   *    root  /home/ubuntu/openreply/deploy/run-cron-job.sh snapshot-followers
